-- DX EXPECTATION (not necessarily spec-required): a digit that is not
-- valid for a radix literal's base, written immediately after the
-- literal, should produce a numeric-literal diagnostic ("invalid digit
-- for base 2 literal", the way Rust/GHC report it), not a type error.
--
-- Today `0b12` lexes as `0b1` followed by the separate literal `2`
-- (greedy binDigits, then `2` cannot start an identifier suffix), so
-- the user sees E_APPLICATION_NONCALLABLE "this expression is not
-- callable / callee type: Integer" — an application error two tokens
-- away from the typo. Same for `0o778` -> `(0o77) 8`. Contrast the
-- existing E_NUMERIC_LITERAL_MALFORMED "expected binary digits" that
-- fires when the FIRST digit is wrong (`0b2`): the trailing-digit case
-- deserves the same literal-shaped report. Spec 6.1.6's grammar is
-- silent on this token boundary (adjacent digit after a numeric
-- literal), so this is a DX pin, not a spec-conformance pin.
--
-- Expected: both bad literals report a malformed-numeric-literal error.
--! mode check
--! assertErrorCount 2
--! assertDiagnostic error E_NUMERIC_LITERAL_MALFORMED

badBin : Integer
let badBin = 0b12

badOct : Integer
let badOct = 0o778
-- STATUS(a0497523): FAIL — `0b12`/`0o778` lex as `(0b1) 2`/`(0o77) 8` and report E_APPLICATION_NONCALLABLE "callee type: Integer" instead of any numeric-literal diagnostic
